  "illustration_prompt": "Create a single publication-quality landscape 3:2 narrative illustration for an adult illustrated English-reading booklet. Finished standalone artwork, not a book mockup, not a collage of multiple unrelated panels. Sophisticated contemporary editorial gouache and watercolor, carefully controlled colored-pencil accents, velvety matte paint, subtle warm paper texture, soft natural daylight. Warm ivory paper dominates; subdued eucalyptus and sage greens structure the composition; restrained buttery lemon-yellow and dusty terracotta accents. Calm, comfortable, refined, credible drawing and natural anatomy. An airy coherent composition with one clear story focal point and at most two supporting details, generous breathing room at all edges and softly feathered painted edges into ivory. Detailed but uncluttered. Historically and scientifically appropriate objects, clothing and setting. Show the particular article's pivotal event or mechanism rather than a generic portrait or generic textbook symbols. Human figures treated with dignity; no grotesque caricature, no sensational violence or visible injuries. For disasters, emphasize the setting, evidence or human response without graphic suffering. For uncertain scientific claims, depict the observations and investigation without presenting debated mechanisms as proven. Absolutely no typography, captions, letters, numbers, logos, watermarks, diagram labels, fake writing or invented charts anywhere. Do not render invisible forces as literal magic. Avoid corporate vector icons, slick 3D rendering and childish cartoon style. High-quality, print-ready visual storytelling, matching a warm ivory-and-sage adult watercolor booklet.\n\nARTICLE-SPECIFIC SCENE:\nA distant, historically grounded view across the Sunda Strait toward Krakatoa during the 1883 eruption. The single focal event is an enormous dense volcanic ash column rising above a low tropical volcanic island and spreading high into a pale ivory sky; render the ash as muted charcoal-sage watercolor, with a restrained dusty-terracotta glow only near the vent. Foreground sea and a spare tropical shoreline give a credible scale and location, without showing settlements being destroyed or people in distress. The island should be a low archipelago silhouette, not a snow-covered conical Alpine mountain. No giant Hollywood fireball, no invented shockwave rings, no arrows, no musical or sound icons, no labels, no fake maps. At most one tiny distant period steamship silhouette as a scale cue, far from danger. A dignified adult editorial illustration of the eruption whose sound and atmospheric disturbance reached distant observers.",
